A1. This Lizzie doesn't stick to her colors. ~ CHAMELEON
A9. Fool. (What the clown makes his hit on.) ~ STOOGE
A11. When they take away the silver standard this is what the copper will do. ~ ARREST
A14. This could easily stop that Manchurian trouble. ~ TRUCE
A15. The only thing on the bill that Dad did not kick about. ~ REBATE
A16. She called her sweetie this because he was so candied. ~ FRANK
A17. Qwzerk(yh&6m"$j50f,g;—sloco." ~ PI
A18. This is always blowing about the farm situation. ~ STRAW
A20. The hand that held a singleton in diamonds when hearts were trump. ~ LEFT
A21. This one leads a life that is intense. ~ ARAB
A23. Lo, the poor Indian! (With reservations.) ~ HOPI
A24. It will not be hard to make a fool out of this one. ~ OAF
A25. You may be sure this will never be in traffic from 9 A. M. to 9 P. M. ~ LETUP
A27. Aw! this is full of beans. ~ POD
A28. Are these unemployed? Not a tall. They're Austin chauffeurs and miniature golf caddies. ~ MIDGETS
A32. Ethyl the dearest gal in any station of life, looks so translucent in this color. ~ RED
A34. The direction in which you are going. ~ TO
A36. This fellow ought to know how to pick a chicken. ~ CONNOISSEUR
A39. Tell us, did Cleo pat this? ~ RA
A41. To make house to house visits. ~ GAD
A42. The birthplace of a great American general. ~ DETROIT
A44. A printer's idea of the open spaces. ~ EMS
A46. Fashion predicts these are going to be the latest things in fur coats. ~ WOMEN
A48. The fellow who is on a liquid diet. ~ SOT
A50. In order to do this, one must be so fisticated. ~ SPAR
A52. An unfavorable will. ~ WONT
A53. Fodder's room. ~ SILO
A54. Impossible to define. ~ SELAH
A56. This is what Lou is. (Abbr.) ~ ST
A57. A book that has been one of the best sellers. ~ BIBLE
A58. To add chicken again to chicken salad in Scotch restaurants. (Careful now!) ~ REVEAL
A60. This sounds appealing. ~ BELLE
A61. What we wish that silver lining would do. ~ EMERGE
A62. What the janitor did to stop a heated argument. ~ COALED
A63. These dates come so close together it will not be hard to remember them. ~ DROMEDARY

D1. When Pola Negri dropped this Mary McCormick picked it up. ~ COUNT
D2. What the Chicago school teachers are putting their investments in. ~ HOCK
D3. Time and time again. ~ AGE
D4. Better mark this personal. ~ ME
D5. Try this if you want to throw a wild one. ~ LARIAT
D6. Turn it either way and it will always be before. ~ ERE
D7. Film features that many Hollywood stars are giving expression to. ~ ORBS
D8. A short way for a poet to get below. ~ NEATH
D9. If this chooses to run next year it will have a wet influence on the land. ~ STREAM
D10. The mad parade. ~ TRAFFIC
D12. Try this to sharpen your wits, appetite, razor or what have you. ~ STROP
D13. A member of a set in China. ~ TEAPOT
D16. The only one who can turn follies into cents. ~ FLO
D17. Mrs. Hoover's pet hobby. ~ PRESIDENT
D19. An unattached live wire. ~ WIDOW
D21. One member of the foursome that had a low score. ~ ALTO
D22. An exploded idea. ~ BURST
D26. This is equal to anything. ~ PEER
D29. K9. ~ DOG
D30. What the little "church mouse" might do to someone's heart. ~ GNAW
D31. Man may do this with money but only nature can do it with reason. ~ ENDOW
D33. Only two can play this. ~ DUO
D35. This is now built with strong columns and many stories. ~ PRESS
D37. What the Scotchman did for Freeport. ~ SENT
D38. This is really laughable. ~ RISIBLE
D40. One of the current measures of the times. ~ AMPERE
D43. What Big Ben did when he went on a strike. ~ TOLLED
D45. We are speaking of something in the Mass. ~ SALEM
D47. This is about as friendly with the Hindu as Japan is with China. ~ MOSLEM
D49. When this one comes out of its hole, it is a sign that things are going to mend and corn is sure to grow. ~ TOE
D51. What father did before he signed the check for his dearest son. ~ RAVED
D53. This may be foolish, but "aren't we all"? ~ SILLY
D55. Frau's (boy) freund. ~ HERR
D57. This has been bruin ever since the stock market crash. ~ BEAR
D59. Well, we are glad this is past. ~ AGO
D60. This often rests on the shoulders of some frail woman. ~ BOA
D62. Two follow-up letters from Abie. (Sounds rather seedy). ~ CD


